{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199 a period marked by significant political and military challenges in the Levant. He was a key figure in the Crusader states navigating complex relationships with the Byzantine Empire the Seljuk Turks and other neighboring powers. Raymond III inherited a county weakened by internal strife and external pressures. His reign focused on consolidating power defending Tripoli's borders and maintaining alliances crucial for survival.\n\n*Early Life and Accession to the County*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1120 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He received military training and was actively involved in county affairs from a young age. Upon his father's death in 1154 Raymond inherited the County of Tripoli a strategically important territory on the coast of Syria. His early reign was characterized by dealing with internal factions and external threats particularly from the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum. He faced challenges in maintaining control over the county's diverse population and securing its economic stability. He solidified his rule through diplomacy and military action often relying on alliances with other Crusader states.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw significant military engagement. He fought against the Seljuk Turks in numerous battles defending Tripoli and its surrounding territories. He participated in Crusades including the Third Crusade where he played a notable role. His military leadership and strategic thinking were instrumental in preserving Tripoli's independence for a considerable period. He also engaged in conflicts with neighboring Muslim rulers seeking to expand his influence and secure trade routes.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nA crucial aspect of Raymond III's rule was his adept diplomacy. He cultivated relationships with various powers including the Byzantine Empire the Kingdom of Jerusalem and other Crusader states. He sought alliances to counter the growing power of the Seljuk Turks and to secure mutual support. His diplomatic efforts aimed to maintain a delicate balance of power in the region. He understood the importance of strategic partnerships in the volatile political landscape of the Levant. Raymond III often negotiated treaties and alliances to ensure Tripoli's safety and prosperity.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 after a long and eventful reign. He is remembered as a capable military leader and a skilled diplomat who successfully defended the County of Tripoli against numerous threats. His efforts helped to preserve Tripoli's independence for several decades during a period of significant instability in the Crusader states. He left behind a county strengthened by his leadership and a legacy of resilience. His reign serves as a testament to the challenges and complexities of ruling a Crusader state in the 12th and 13th centuries. He is considered one of the most important Counts of Tripoli and his contributions to the defense of the Crusader states are widely recognized.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0011152960360050201}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2664777338504791}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.12502680718898773}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.08393791317939758}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00027021096320822835}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.6702524337451905e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -6.6756979322235566e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.3854863431770355e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0029883987735956907}, {"token": 